# Geography

{{ statement() }}

A metropolitan area is a list of counties, and the list changes. The OMB delineation files committed under `data/delineations` give the county to area mapping for every vintage the window crosses ({{ v('data.omb.vintages') }} vintages, the latest {{ v('data.omb.latest_vintage') }}, with {{ v('data.omb.metropolitan_areas') }} metropolitan and {{ v('data.omb.micropolitan_areas') }} micropolitan areas). The warehouse carries every area under its latest code: {{ v('data.omb.earlier_codes') }} earlier codes are not latest codes, {{ v('data.omb.with_alias') }} of them resolve to a latest area through the alias table (`data/derived/area_aliases.parquet`: {{ v('data.omb.recoded') }} went on under a new code, {{ v('data.omb.merged') }} were absorbed into a neighbour) and {{ v('data.omb.without_alias') }} were dissolved or split and stay outside the metro universe. An earlier code aliases to the latest area that holds more than half of its counties; one that no latest area holds more than half of has no alias.

## How each source is mapped

- **FHFA** publishes by area code and by metropolitan division on the current definitions; the divisions are rolled up to the area, weighted by owner households (`int_fhfa_area`).
- **ACS** publishes by area code for the vintage in force in the release year; the code is carried through the alias table to the latest code (`int_acs_resolved`). When a merged area and the area that absorbed it both publish in one year, the absorbing area's row is kept and the other is counted, so each latest area has one row per year and its series carries a step where the merge happened.
- **Permits, population and the loans** are county files; every county is placed in the area it belongs to on the latest delineation, for every year, so a metro is the same counties throughout and its series never steps where a county moved (`int_county_vintage_map`, `int_county_year`). A county the latest delineation dropped from every area maps nowhere. A county whose code was retired (Connecticut's counties, replaced by planning regions) maps through the last vintage that carried it, so its history reaches the area that goes on under the latest code.
- **HMDA** carries the county of the property; the loan is placed through that county, the derived area code the file also carries is only a check, and a loan whose county is in no area and whose code is blank or the non metro sentinel is flagged by the `metro_unresolved` rule and kept.
- **Puerto Rico** has four metropolitan areas in the delineation with census rows and loans but no FHFA index, so they appear in the explorer's census tables and in no anchored chapter; the warehouse test that asks for a year of prices in every large metropolitan area leaves them out by name.
- **HUD** fair market rents and income limits are published by county and rolled up to the area the same way, weighted by population; the HUD area to metro map is committed from the HUD area definition file of the latest year (`data/hud_area_map.csv`).

## Tests

Every county maps to exactly one area per vintage (a planted county in two areas is rejected by the `county_in_two_areas` rule and its test); the alias function names a recode, a merge, a dissolution and a split on a planted crosswalk; every alias points at one latest code, and an earlier code resolves to the same latest area in every vintage that carries it (`assert_every_alias_points_at_one_latest_code`); the county roll up on the latest delineation reproduces the Bureau's own published metropolitan and micropolitan population totals of the latest vintage within one tenth of one percent for every area and year both carry (`assert_county_roll_up_matches_published_metro_totals`), which is the reconciliation across the {{ v('data.omb.latest_vintage') }} recomposition; a loan in a delineation county resolves without a reported area code and a loan in no such county with no code is flagged (`test_a_loan_in_a_metro_county_resolves_without_a_reported_code`). The crosswalk tests live in `tests/data/test_contracts.py` and in the warehouse schema and `warehouse/tests`.

## What the recomposition moves

The latest delineation renames and recomposes areas. On the county side nothing moves, because every year is on the latest definition. On the census side an area that absorbed a neighbour shows a step in its household count in the year of the merge that is geography, not growth; the chapters that compare years use the anchored index, which FHFA publishes on the same fixed definition, for the price side of every comparison, and the household counts only as weights.
